"Practical Verified Computation with Streaming Interactive Proofs (https://arxiv.org/html/1105.2003v5)\nciteturn10view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn9view1\",\"pattern\":\"repeated squaring\"}); Total lines: 800\nL698: The circuit $C$ consists of two stages: the first stage computes the adjacency matrix of the configuration graph of $T$ on input $x$, which requires just a single layer of gates, while the second stage determines whether there is a path from the starting configuration of $T$ on input $x$ to the accepting configuration. The second stage determines whether such a path exists by a process resembling repeated squaring of the adjacency matrix of $T$.\nL701: The configuration graph $G$ of $T$ is a directed acyclic graph with $2^{g(n)}$ nodes, one for each configuration of $T$, and an edge from $u$ to $v$ if $T$ can move in one step from configuration $u$ to configuration $v$. We include self-loops in this graph.\nL702: As in the full version of [【42†19】], let $B_{x}$ denote the adjacency matrix of $T$. The circuit $C$ first computes the entries of $B_{x}$, and then computes $\\log t(n)$ matrices $B_{\\log t(n)},\\dots,B_{0}$, where the $(u,v)$’th entry of $B_{p}$ is 1 if there is a path of length at most $2^{\\log t(n)-p}$ from $u$ to $v$ in $G$.\nL703: The matrix $B_{p}$ is obtained from $B_{p+1}$ by a process resembling repeated squaring of $B_{x}$ using naive matrix multiplication.^{7}^{7} 7 More specifically, $B_{p}[u,v]=1+\\prod_{w\\in\\{0,1\\}^{g(n)}}\\left(1+B_{p}[u,w]B_{p}[w,v]\\right),$ where all arithmetic is done over an extension field of $\\mathbb{F}_{2}$.\nL704: The wiring structure of this stage of the circuit is similar to that for naive matrix-vector multiplication, and it is straightforward to observe that the multilinear extensions of $add_{i}$ and $mult_{i}$ for these layers can be evaluated in $O(\\log S(n))$ time and using $O(\\log S(n))$ words of space. We omit these details for brevity.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPractical Verified Computation with Streaming Interactive Proofs (https://arxiv.org/html/1105.2003v5)\nciteturn10view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn9view1\",\"pattern\":\"wiring\"}); Total lines: 800\nL265: We abstract the notion of a “wiring predicate”, which encodes which pairs of wires from layer $i-1$ are connected to a given gate at layer $i$. Each iteration $i$ consists of an application of the standard sum-check protocol [【49†25】, 【50†33】] to a $3v$-variate polynomial $f_{i}$ based on the wiring predicate. There is some flexibility in choosing the specific polynomial $f_{i}$ to use.\nL266: This is because the definition of $f_{i}$ involves a low-degree extension of the circuit’s wiring predicate, and there are many such low-degree extensions to choose from.\nL267: A polynomial is said to be multilinear if it has degree at most one in each variable. The results in this section rely critically on the observation that the honest prover’s computation in the protocol of [【42†19】] can be greatly simplified if we use the multilinear extension of the circuit’s wiring predicate.^{4}^{4} 4 There are other reasons why using the multilinear extension is desirable. For example, the communication cost of the protocol is proportional to the degree of the extension used.\nL268: Details of this observation follow.\nL269: As already mentioned, at iteration $i$ of the protocol of [【42†19】], the sum-check protocol is applied to the $3v$-variate polynomial $f_{i}$. In the $j$’th round of this sum-check protocol, $\\mathcal{P}$ is required to send the univariate polynomial\nL270: \nL271:  | $\\displaystyle g_{j}(X_{j})$  | $\\displaystyle\\!=\\!\\sum_{(x_{j+1},\\dots,x_{3v})\\in\\{0,1\\}^{3v-j}}f_{i}(r^{(i)}_{1},\\dots,r^{(i)}_{j-1},X_{j},x_{j+1},\\dots,x_{3v}).$  |\nL272: The sum defining $g_{j}$ involves as many as $n^{3}$ terms, and thus a naive implementation of $\\mathcal{P}$ would require $\\Omega(n^{3})$ time per iteration of the protocol. However, we observe that if the multilinear extension of the circuit’s wiring predicate is used in the definition of $f_{i}$, then each gate at layer $i-1$ contributes to exactly one term in the sum defining $g_{j}$, as does each gate at layer $i$.\nL273: Thus, the polynomial $g_{j}$ can be computed with a single pass over the gates at layer $i-1$, and a single pass over the gates at layer $i$. As the sum-check protocol requires $O(v)=O(\\log S(n))$ messages for each layer of the circuit, $\\mathcal{P}$ requires logarithmically many passes over each layer of the circuit in total.\nL274: A complication in applying the above observation is that $\\mathcal{V}$ must process the circuit in order to pull out information about its structure necessary to check the validity of $\\mathcal{P}$’s messages. Specifically, each application of the sum-check protocol requires $\\mathcal{V}$ to evaluate the multilinear extension of the wiring predicate of the circuit at a random point.\nL275: Theorem 【76†3.2】 follows from the fact that for any log-space uniform circuit, $\\mathcal{V}$ can evaluate the multilinear extension of the wiring predicate at any point using space $O(\\log S(n))$. We present detailed proofs and discussions of the following theorems in Appendix 【27†A】.\nL276: ###### Theorem 3.2\nL277: \nL278: For any log-space uniform circuit of size $S(n)$, $\\mathcal{P}$ requires $O(S(n)\\log S(n))$ time to implement the protocol of Theorem 【75†3.1】 over the entire execution, and $\\mathcal{V}$ requires space $O(\\log S(n))$.\nL279: Moreover, because the circuit’s wiring predicate is independent of the input, we can separate $\\mathcal{V}$’s computation into an offline non-interactive preprocessing phase, which occurs before the data stream is seen, and an online interactive phase which occurs after both $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{V}$ have seen the input.\nL280: This is similar to [【42†19】, Theorem 4], and ensures that $\\mathcal{V}$ is space-efficient (but may require time $\\mathrm{poly}(S(n))$) during the offline phase, and that $\\mathcal{V}$ is both time- and space-efficient in the online interactive phase. In order to determine which circuit to use, $\\mathcal{V}$ does need to know (an upper bound on) the length of the input during the preprocessing phase.\nL281: ###### Theorem 3.3\nL282: For any log-space uniform circuit of size $S(n)$, $\\mathcal{P}$ requires $O(S(n)\\log S(n))$ time to implement the protocol of Theorem 【75†3.1】 over the entire execution.\nL283: $\\mathcal{V}$ requires space $O(d(n)\\log S(n))$ and time $O(\\mathrm{poly}(S(n)))$ in a non-interactive, data-independent preprocessing phase, and only requires space $O(d(n)\\log S(n))$ and time $O(n\\log n+d(n)\\log S(n))$ in an online interactive phase, where the $O(n\\log n)$ term is due to the time required to evaluate the low-degree extension of the input at a point.\nL284: Finally, Theorem 【78†3.4】 follows by assuming $\\mathcal{V}$ can evaluate the multilinear extension of the wiring predicate quickly. A formal statement of Theorem 【78†3.4】 is in Appendix 【27†A】. We believe that the hypothesis of Theorem 【78†3.4】 is extremely mild, and we discuss this point at length in Appendix 【27†A】, identifying a diverse array of circuits to which Theorem 【78†3.4】 applies.\nL285: Moreover, the solutions we adopt in our circuit-checking experiments for $F_{2}$, $F_{0}$, and PMwW correspond to Theorem 【78†3.4】, and are both space- and time-efficient for the verifier.\nL286: ###### Theorem 3.4\nL287: (informal) Let $C$ be any log-space uniform circuit of size $S(n)$ and depth $d(n)$, and assume there exists a $O(\\log S(n))$-space, $\\mathrm{poly}(\\log S(n))$-time algorithm for evaluating the multilinear extension of $C$’s wiring predicate at a point.\nL288: Then in order to to implement the protocol of Theorem 【75†3.1】 applied to $C$, $\\mathcal{P}$ requires $O(S(n)\\log S(n))$ time, and $\\mathcal{V}$ requires space $O(\\log S(n))$ and time $O(n\\log n+d(n)\\mathrm{poly}(\\log S(n)))$, where the $O(n\\log n)$ term is due to the time required to evaluate the low-degree extension of the input at a point.\nL532: More specifically, the key observation is that the multilinear extension of the wiring predicate acts as a sum of variable-wise indicator functions on boolean-valued variables, with one indicator function for each gate at the layer of interest.\nL535: #### A.2.2 Decomposing $\\tilde{\\text{add}}_{i}$ and $\\tilde{\\text{mult}}_{i}$ as Sums of Variable-wise Indicator Functions\nL536: \nL537: Since $\\tilde{\\text{add}}_{i}$ and $\\tilde{\\text{mult}}_{i}$ are the multilinear extensions of the wiring predicate, we can write them explicitly as follows.\nL597: In essence, Theorem 【104†A.1】 applies to any circuit with a “highly regular” wiring pattern; this explains why it applies to such a wide array of circuits. The details in the remainder of the section grow lengthy at times, but the thesis is clear: Theorem 【104†A.1】 applies to most circuits that arise in both practical applications and theoretical constructions.\nL598: #### A.4.1 Wiring Predicates for $F_{2}$, $F_{0}$, PMwW, and MVMult\nL599: \nL600: 【105†Image: Refer to caption】 Figure 4: A circuit for $F_{2}$ on 4 inputs.\nL601: \nL602: We demonstrate that Theorem 【104†A.1】 applies to all four circuits described in Section 【13†3】.\nL603: \nL604:   1. 1.\nL622: $F_{0}$: Recall that for each of the $n$ inputs $a_{i}$, the circuit for $F_{0}$ from Section 【13†3】 computes $a_{i}^{p-1}$ via $O(\\log p)$ multiplications, and then sums the results via a binary tree of addition gates. We have already seen the wiring predicate for binary trees, so here we only sketch the wiring predicate for the $a_{i}^{p-1}$ computation, omitting some details for brevity.\nL626: At a high level then, the wiring predicate $mult_{i}(p,\\omega_{1},\\omega_{2})$ tests equality of $\\omega_{1}$ and $\\omega_{2}$ with two strings that depend on the parity of $p$, as even values of $p$ correspond to gates computing $a^{2^{i}}$ while odd values correspond to gates computing $\\prod_{j=1}^{i-1}a^{2^{i-1}}$. Thus, we may write\nL627: \nL628:  | $$\\tilde{\\text{mult}}_{i}(p,\\omega_{1},\\omega_{2})\\!=\\!(1-p_{1})\\chi_{\\text{even}}(p,\\omega_{1},\\omega_{2})\\!+\\!p_{1}\\chi_{\\text{odd}}(p,\\omega_{1},\\omega_{2}),$$  |\nL629: where $\\chi_{\\text{odd}}$ and $\\chi_{\\text{even}}$ are multilinear extensions of the appropriate equality predicates, which do not depend on $p_{1}$ (we omit a precise definition of $\\chi_{\\text{odd}}$ and $\\chi_{\\text{even}}$ for brevity). This can clearly be evaluated in $O(v)$ time and space.\nL630: \nL631:   3. 3.\nL632: \nL633: PMwW: The circuit for PMwW is similar to that for $F_{0}$  so we omit the details for this circuit.\nL634: \nL635:   4. 4.\nL636: MVMult: The circuit described in Section 【13†3】 for MVMult computes $(A\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{b})_{i}$ for all $1\\leq i\\leq n$, and then applies the circuit for $F_{0}$ to the result. We have already sketched the wiring predicate for $F_{0}$, so we need only describe the wiring predicate of the circuit $C$ computing $(A\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{b})_{i}$ for all $1\\leq i\\leq n$. For presentation purposes, we only describe the wiring predicate for a circuit $C^{\\prime}$ which computes $(A\\mathbf{x})_{i}$ for all $i$.\nL637: The wiring predicate for $C^{\\prime}$ is simpler than that of $C$, since $C$ requires some extra gates to “propagate” the entries of $\\mathbf{b}$ up to the final layer of the circuit, where they are finally used to compute $(A\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{b})_{i}$ for all $1\\leq i\\leq n$. We emphasize that Theorem 【104†A.1】 applies to the circuit $C$ as well.\nL638: Assume $n$ is a power of 2. To simplify the wiring predicate of $C^{\\prime}$, we will treat $C^{\\prime}$ as having $2n^{2}$ inputs, where the first $n^{2}$ inputs of $C^{\\prime}$ are the entries of $A$ in row-major order; and the last $n$ inputs are the entries of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$, with all the remaining inputs (between $n^{2}$ and $2n^{2}-n$) set to 0 and ignored in subsequent layers.\nL639: We emphasize that this convention does not increase the costs to either $\\mathcal{P}$ or $\\mathcal{V}$ in the protocol applied to $C^{\\prime}$.\nL656: Subsequent layers of $C^{\\prime}$ compute $\\sum_{j=1}^{n}A_{ij}x_{j}$ for each $1\\leq i\\leq n$, which is performed via a binary tree of addition gates for each $i$. We have already described the predicate for this wiring pattern in the paragraph on $F_{2}$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpeeding Up Sum-Check Proving (Extended Version) (https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/587)\nciteturn10view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn9view2\",\"pattern\":\"History\"}); Total lines: 82\nL49: Available format(s)\nL50:     【17†PDF】\nL51: Category\nL52:     【18†Applications】\nL53: Publication info\nL54:     Preprint.\nL55: Keywords\nL56:     【19†sum-check】【20†snark】【21†zkvm】【22†delayed-reduction】【23†jolt】【24†spartan】【25†twist-shout】\nL57: Contact author(s)\nL58:     qvd @ andrew cmu edu\nL59: zd2131 @ nyu edu\nL60: suyash @ ingonyama com\nL61: yuval domb @ gmail com\nL62: justin r thaler @ gmail com\nL63: History\nL64:     2026-03-25: approved\nL65:     2026-03-24: received\nL66:     【26†See all versions】\nL67: Short URL\nL68:     【27†https://ia.cr/2026/587†ia.cr】\nL69: License\nL70: \nL71: 【28†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nchore(deps): adopt published SP1 6.10.0 patches (#3027) · succinctlabs/sp1@d83ed91 · GitHub (https://github.com/succinctlabs/sp1/commit/d83ed91f96701209613720708f36ffce0e98ed33)\nciteturn10view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn8view1\",\"pattern\":